# Sound-alike: "encase" (to enclose) written for "in case". Examples are ours.
# why: "Encase" means to put something inside a case or cover. "In case" (two words) means "if it happens that" or "as a precaution".
expr main <([(just encase), (encase of)]), encase>

let message "`Encase` means to enclose something; here it's `in case`."
let description "Fixes `just encase it rains` → `just in case it rains`."
let kind "Malapropism"
let becomes "in case"
let strategy "MatchCase"

test "Take an umbrella just encase it rains." "Take an umbrella just in case it rains."
test "Encase of fire, use the stairs." "In case of fire, use the stairs."

allows "Encase the wires in plastic before burying them."
allows "Take an umbrella just in case it rains."
